/* Unix file permissions, the way you would actually write them.

   This is what the `bits` type is for. A mode like 0o755 is not the quantity seven hundred and
   fifty-five — it is three permission triples, and treating it as a decimal was the category
   error that made bit work painful before this type existed.

   Note what does NOT appear anywhere below: no divide-and-modulo standing in for a mask, no
   negative numbers, and no conversion back and forth to read a single bit. */

Pull a book on collections.

   /* Each permission is one bit.
      Written in octal because the grouping is the meaning:
      one digit per triple, owner then group then other. */
   Define owner-read     as 0o400.
   Define owner-write    as 0o200.
   Define owner-execute  as 0o100.
   Define group-read     as 0o040.
   Define group-write    as 0o020.
   Define group-execute  as 0o010.
   Define other-read     as 0o004.
   Define other-write    as 0o002.
   Define other-execute  as 0o001.

   /* Setting bits is `or`; the accumulator goes on the left, so the result stays octal. */
   Define mode as owner-read or owner-write or owner-execute
               or group-read or group-execute
               or other-read or other-execute.

   State "mode:        {mode converted to text}".
   State "as a number: {mode converted to number converted to text}".
   State "in binary:   {mode converted to number converted to binary converted to text}".

   /* Testing a bit is `and` — non-zero means the bit was set. */
   Bind fact to allows, given (the bits m, the bits flag), (m and flag) is not 0o000.

   State "".
   State "owner may write:  {cast allows on (mode, owner-write) converted to text}".
   State "group may write:  {cast allows on (mode, group-write) converted to text}".
   State "other may execute: {cast allows on (mode, other-execute) converted to text}".

   /* Clearing a bit is `and not` — the only clean way to unset one, and the reason `not`
      had to exist on bits rather than being dropped for being surprising. */
   Define tightened as mode and not other-read and not other-execute.
   State "".
   State "after removing other's read and execute: {tightened converted to text}".

   /* Shifting moves a triple into position. The amount is a NUMBER — it counts places, which
      is a quantity, not a pattern. */
   Define owner-triple as 0o7.
   State "".
   State "0o7 shifted into the owner triple: {(owner-triple shifted left by 6) converted to text}".

   /* Building a mask: (1 << n) - 1, the standard idiom. */
   Define low-six as (0o1 shifted left by 6) - 0o1.
   State "the low six bits: {low-six converted to text}".
   State "mode within them: {(mode and low-six) converted to text}".

   /* A umask subtracts permissions, which is exactly `and not`. */
   Define umask as 0o022.
   Define after-umask as 0o777 and not umask.
   State "".
   State "0o777 with a 0o022 umask: {after-umask converted to text}".
Done.
